{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: the remote-link lane needs a repair child. Codex's changes-requested verdict d731273a on head 7da8b753 is ratified at 480e989b, and a ratified verdict authorizes nothing — the governing row is your child 48ec44db, so the authority to touch the head is yours to grant. I am asking before implementing rather than after, which is the thing I got wrong three times on this same lane.\n\nTHE FIRST BLOCKER IS MINE AND IT IS THE SERIOUS ONE. I made `git config --local` the fix for reading only the repository's own remotes, and then published an artifact and an architecture-page contract saying the read is repository-local. `--local` bounds which configuration file is consulted. It does not bound which repository Git resolves. I reproduced it: two repositories, GIT_COMMON_DIR pointed at the second, and `git --no-optional-locks -C victim config --local --null --get-regexp '^remote\\..*\\.url'` returned the attacker repository's remote, exit 0. Every other defence on that path — the http/https allowlists, the 64 KiB and 64-remote bounds, the browser's refusal of credentials and delimiters — sits downstream of a value that could already have come from another repository. Codex found this; I did not, and I had asserted the opposite in a published artifact.\n\nTHE SECOND IS NARROWER. net/url.Parse drops a bare trailing \"#\", so Go admits \"https://host/repo.git#\" and serializes it without the delimiter, while the browser's URL preserves it and repolink.ts refuses it. Confirmed both halves directly. Nothing is exploitable — the browser fails closed — but the prior verdict and the pages I published say a query or fragment input is declined and the field omitted, and it is not: it is silently normalised. Codex's suggested shape, rejecting the raw delimiters before parsing, is also simpler than what is there.\n\nWHY THE ARCHITECTURE PAGE HAS TO MOVE IN THE SAME HEAD. docs/reference/architecture.md at 7da8b753 records \"repository-local configuration only\" as a layer contract. That sentence is false as written, so this is not a code repair with a documentation follow-up; the contract and the code have to land together or the page keeps asserting a property the code does not have.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. Not a new lane and not a widening of substance. The seven artifacts, the head and the tree are otherwise sound — codex's verdict says the allowlists, userinfo refusal, bounds, noopener/noreferrer and name-before-filter behaviour are all correct, and confirms the branch, tree, provenance, artifact paths and gates. Two defects, one page, one head.\n\nIf you would rather this went to a different actor, say so and I will hand over what I have; I have the reproduction for both defects ready either way.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Repair the two blockers in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict d731273a on head 7da8b753, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. (1) Bind the remote read to the resolved repository so inherited Git routing variables cannot redirect it, reusing the existing environment-sanitization model rather than adding a second repository resolver, and prove by regression that LocalRepo and /v0/worktrees cannot expose a remote injected through GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE or GIT_COMMON_DIR. (2) Reject raw query and fragment delimiters before parsing on the Go side, with a bare-# regression beside the existing bare-? case, so the Go and browser policies stop diverging. Correct docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head: its \"repository-local configuration only\" contract is false as written and must move with the code. Run the full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset, and state results from runs watched to exit. Show by mutation that each added regression fails when its guard is broken. Publish fresh exact-head artifacts superseding the 7da8b753 set at the same seven paths, each resting on the promise to this request, plus any further path the repair actually changes — the artifact set follows the diff, per your ruling 0139dd87. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this request. Do not merge.","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}